            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** From the ductwork for the downdraft range hood fan - investigation in the crawlspace showed that this duct would be directly open, but is currently poorly sealed. 



** A vent coming up from the crawlspace for the wood stove



** Around the windows and at the old wood front door



** At the connections between walls, floors, and ceilings, as is typical in older houses



** The current chimney and 8" combustion air supply pipe for the furnace are causing a significant amount of air leakage. See the section on your heating system for information about electronic dampers. 



** In general, there seems to be a lot of air leakage coming from unsealed penetrations through the floor. While modelling suggests that the savings from adding insulation to the floor will be very low, I do recommend air sealing any penetrations from the crawlspace. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $120 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. This upgrade would also allow for air sealing work to be done at the same time. 



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $590 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 14.9 GJ.</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>



The exposed floor of the porch and front alcove of this home appear to be 2x10 joists with 7 inches (R32) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation below these joists, at the same time as adding the same R15 insulation to the crawlspace as described in that section of the report. 



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $16 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ.</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x10 joists with R32 insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation below the joists. 



I recommend that prior to committing to this upgrade, you confirm the amount of insulation that is already in the floor above the crawlspace, as well as whether or not there is an air barrier in the assembly. I also recommend that you perform some air sealing around the ducts and pipe penetrations into the crawlspace, and throughout the crawlspace. 



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation below the joists of the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially save $140 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 03.6 GJ.

</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house are older double pane wood windows that are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. 



In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



The front door is a solid wood door, with no insulation. I have not modeled this upgrade, but I recommend that you replace it with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone. 



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $890 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 22.3 GJ. </windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified direct vent, non-condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Since the direct vent furnace will not require a combustion air supply but your oil fired hot water tank will, I recommend that a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of the hot water tank are satisfied if you upgrade the furnace. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the hot water tank is operating.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with an 87% AFUE direct vent, non-condensing oil furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $190 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. 



Our currrent advice recommends a 95% efficient, direct vent, condensing oil furnace, which will result in additional savings above what is listed above. However, I selected this type of furnace based on what is likely to be available in the community of Tulita. I recommend that you reach out to see what is available before making your final selection. </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Thermal Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal, and LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



I recommend that all lighting in this home be replaced with LED lighting if not done already. This includes exterior lighting. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 12.3 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil furnace and wood stove are both susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of this home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



This home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood that is vented into the crawlspace

        - one bathroom fan 

        - a clothes dryer



I recommend installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Tulita



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Tulita:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil - $1.51 per liter 

Wood - $735 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested



